<Concept title="Movement & Lock-on" section="Mechanics">
  <p><strong>Combat prototype (#775), November 10, 2026.</strong> The playable slice provides normal view, toggleable shoulder aiming and explicit enemy lock/switching with handgun, rifle, mechgun, saber, sword, daggers, rod and wand. Force test palettes provide Foie, Barta and Resta; other technique targeting remains follow-up work. This is an intentional control experiment, not original-game behavior parity.</p>
  <h2>Scope and launch</h2>
  <p>Run <code>python3 scripts/tools/run_ranger_aim.py</code> for a separate Ranger test save in the Coliseum. Add <code>--female</code> for RAmarl or <code>--weapon rifle</code> / <code>--weapon mechgun</code>. Use <code>--class humar --weapon saber</code> or <code>--class fomar --weapon rod</code> to launch those classes directly. F1 cycles eight curated Ranger/Hunter/Force loadouts; F2 rebuilds the current fixture, including defeated enemies. Both use the isolated prototype save. Normal launches retain their existing controls; <code>PSZ_AIM_PROTOTYPE=1</code> enables the experiment. Grades remain unchanged pending owner playtest.</p>
  <h2>Controls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Z toggles shoulder aim. Q toggles explicit enemy lock in normal view; Q does nothing in shoulder view. Tab cycles visible targets while locked. Z switches from lock to shoulder aim and clears the selected enemy. Mouse controls yaw and pitch only in shoulder view; right stick / camera keys remain available in normal view; lock view follows the selected enemy. Mouse MUST be captured only in shoulder view. Exiting shoulder view, menus and focus loss release it. Normal view and enemy lock leave the cursor free; cursor movement MUST NOT rotate the normal-view camera. Prototype controls suppress legacy camera recenter/drag bindings.</li>
    <li>WASD / left stick moves independently of facing at 4.5 units/sec (75% of normal run speed), with analog magnitude preserved and diagonals capped. Sideways and backward travel MUST use the native <code>_stp_lr</code> / <code>_stp_fb</code> clips, reversed for the opposite direction. Female libraries contain shared male-named step clips; select their actual names. Strip root translation so motion is owned by the character controller.</li>
    <li>Left/right/middle click dispatch palette slots 1/2/3 through their shared action handlers, including held technique charging and palette-page selection. J / west face retains its existing palette binding. Space / left shoulder dodges. Z/Q/Tab mode selection is keyboard-only in this iteration; existing controller movement, camera and attacks remain available. Input-owning overlays MUST take precedence.</li>
    <li>The fixture starts with stationary enemies at normal health: T toggles their AI and R resets positions/HP of surviving enemies. Only automated checks use inflated target health. Restart the launcher to rebuild the entire fixture.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Movement and commitment</h2>
  <p>While freely moving in stance, facing MUST follow aim yaw or the selected enemy and movement MUST retain the normal floor, wall, step and edge constraints. No stance sprint. Accepted attacks retain their existing movement commitment, hit timing and recovery. Attack admission/queued facing MUST use aim rather than strafe direction. Dodge MUST take the held travel direction (facing without input), keep it throughout the roll, and retain existing admission/damage rules. The camera may turn during a roll without bending its path. The prototype fits dodge movement and recovery to the native roll clip duration, integrating the displacement curve across elapsed time; it MUST NOT enter idle midway through a still-moving roll. A completed roll MUST restore the pre-roll character facing on the stand-up frame, before normal aiming resumes; interrupted rolls MUST discard that saved facing.</p>
  <h2>Movement sounds</h2>
  <p>In the prototype, footsteps MUST follow animation cues instead of a fixed wall-clock interval: one cue per full cycle while aiming/locked, two alternating cues per cycle in normal movement. Aiming/locked footsteps use -16 dB instead of -8 dB and an 1800 Hz low-pass filter routed into the SFX bus; other sounds MUST remain unaffected. Reverse step playback and loop wrap MUST retain alternating cues; changing clips/direction MUST NOT emit a synthetic starting step. A stopped animation, airborne player, idle state or blocked horizontal motion MUST NOT produce footsteps. The existing terrain/race sound selection is retained. Contact phases are provisional and need listening/visual playtesting across rigs.</p>
  <h2>Class testing</h2>
  <p>F1 cycles RAmar/handgun, RAmarl/rifle, RAcast/mechgun, HUmar/saber, HUmarl/sword, HUnewearl/dagger, FOmar/rod and FOmarl/wand. Melee lock controls facing without granting reach; the existing melee contact shape and combo windows remain authoritative. Force front palette uses left click Foie, right click Barta and middle click Resta. Hold Shift for the back palette: attack, strong attack and Monofluid; holding retains the existing charged variants. Base Foie and Barta MUST aim through the shoulder ray or selected lock target, preserve their existing ranges and stop at cover. Barta remains a ground-traveling projectile. Area/charged techniques retain their existing behaviors. F2 restarts the selected loadout.</p>
  <h2>Three-hit chains while aiming</h2>
  <p>Each left-click MUST enter the shared attack/combo path. Timing a second and third press in the existing weapon-specific accept windows queues attacks 2 and 3 without releasing aim. Aiming MUST NOT add automatic fire, bypass early-press fumbles, create a fourth step or cancel recovery. Each step retains direct reticle targeting or the explicitly selected enemy at projectile release. Projectiles do not home.</p>
  <p>Timing and recovery feedback MUST appear as the normal shrinking circles around action-palette slots, with no timing bars, counters, prompts or timing colors near the cursor. Attack circles use the actual weapon-specific accept window and queued state; technique charge circles follow the held slot and turn green when charged. Cast/dodge recovery circles follow their actual animation/action clocks on combat slots and disappear at completion. The crosshair and selected-enemy marker remain aim-only indicators.</p>
  <h2>Shoulder camera and direct shots</h2>
  <p>Initial tuning: 3 units behind, 0.6 units right, 1.5-unit look height, existing 44° FOV and a 0.15-second framing blend. Sphere clearance against world geometry takes precedence over the shoulder offset. A center crosshair marks direct aim, distinct from existing enemy cone markers. Exiting shoulder aim preserves camera yaw/pitch.</p>
  <p>At shot release, cast the center camera ray to the first world/hurtbox contact or a distant point. Fire from the player's logical weapon origin (one unit above the feet) toward that point, preserving the weapon's travel-range cap and existing projectile hit semantics. Aimed shots MUST NOT snap to the nearest enemy. They MUST stop at world cover, including initial overlap and obstacles crossed in a single physics step; an earlier enemy contact still wins. Shots do not home. Ordinary exploration shots retain nearest-cone-target assistance. This slice uses the logical origin rather than an animated barrel socket.</p>
  <h2>Explicit lock</h2>
  <p>Q chooses the living, unobstructed enemy nearest screen center within 20 units. Tab cycles visible candidates in screen order. Selection MUST persist as enemies move; a nearer enemy MUST NOT steal it. Normal camera distance is retained while following the selected enemy, offset one unit right so the player does not hide the target, with world clearance. WASD strafes relative to the selected enemy. A yellow ring identifies the selected target, and the shared three-hit timing indicator remains visible.</p>
  <p>Lock clears when the enemy dies, leaves the tree, moves beyond 24 units, or stays behind world cover for 0.5 seconds. It MUST NOT automatically retarget. Shots aim at the selected enemy's collision center at release while retaining weapon range and world collision; lock does not grant extra reach or homing.</p>
  <h2>Lifecycle</h2>
  <p>Menus, cutscenes, death, focus loss and unsupported weapons clear both targeting modes. Closing a blocked state MUST NOT restore a stale toggle; another key press is required. Key repeat MUST NOT toggle modes. Captured mouse state MUST restore for menus, loss of focus or leaving the scene. Shop focus keeps camera ownership. The feature is desktop/controller only in this slice; touch controls are not enabled.</p>
  <h2>Validation</h2>
  <p><code>combat_aim_tests.gd</code> runs in the seeded test runner and checks toggle clearing, travel axes, diagonal caps and analog movement. <code>run_ranger_aim.py --check</code> drives the actual Ranger, native clips, physics and projectile release: all four travel directions, fixed dodge heading, no dodge cancellation, overlay clearing, key-repeat rejection, explicit target selection/switching/retention, target death/range cleanup, three-hit chains in both modes, shot commitment, off-center nearest-target rejection, cover and mode exit. The existing projectile physics probe checks collision ordering and range boundaries. Rendered checks and owner testing assess framing and animation feel; automated checks do not establish those.</p>

  <h2>Implemented by</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>scripts/3d/player/player.gd</code> (Player) — locomotion lives in <code>_handle_movement</code> (camera-relative input, walk/run/sprint speeds and the walk-to-run timer, turn-speed scaling) with floor/edge checks in <code>_can_move_to</code> / <code>_has_floor_at</code> and step climbing in <code>_apply_step_up</code>; target acquisition (the forward targeting box and reticles that lock-on rides on) is in <code>_update_combat_targets</code>.</li>
    <li><code>scripts/3d/camera/orbit_camera.gd</code> (OrbitCamera node) — the orbit follow camera whose basis <code>_handle_movement</code> reads to make movement camera-relative; exposes <code>get_camera_rotation()</code> / <code>set_camera_rotation()</code> and recenters behind the player. Its behavior contract lives in <a href={siteUrl('/mechanics/camera')}>Camera</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The full state machine these movement states belong to is documented in <a href={siteUrl('/states/player-state')}>Player State Machine</a>.</p>

